[82.3, "o", "\r\nLet me now check the BloomPass for the pattern of swallowing init errors.\r\n"]
[83.8, "o", "\u001b[2m  [79,006 ctx | 3 out]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[84.6, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Read\u001b[0m \u001b[2mRendering/BloomPass.swift\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[84.75, "o", "\r\nNow I have all the information I need. Let me also check the `_currentDrawable` potential issue more carefully and then write the findings.\r\n"]
[86.25, "o", "\u001b[2m  [80,198 ctx | 2 out]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[87.05, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Grep\u001b[0m \u001b[2m/luminanceSumBuffers|GPU.*latency|readback|shared/\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[87.2, "o", "\r\nOne more check -- I want to verify if the AccessibilityClampPass GPU readback has a synchronization issue. The CPU reads the previous frame's buffer, but the GPU command buffer for that previous frame might not have completed yet.\r\n"]
[88.7, "o", "\u001b[2m  [80,584 ctx | 1 out]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[89.5, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Read\u001b[0m \u001b[2mRendering/AccessibilityClampPass.swift\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[89.65, "o", "\r\nThis is important. The AccessibilityClampPass reads `luminanceSumBuffers[readIndex]` on the CPU at the time of encoding, but the GPU work that writes to that buffer was encoded in the *previous* frame's command buffer. With triple-buffering (3 frames in flight), when frame N is being encoded, frame N-1's command buffer might not have completed yet. The double-buffering (2 buffers) means frame N reads the buffer written by frame N-1, but if 3 frames are in flight, frame N-1 could still be executing on the GPU. Let me verify the semaphore value.\r\n"]
[91.15, "o", "\u001b[2m  [81,469 ctx | 1 out]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[91.95, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Grep\u001b[0m \u001b[2m/semaphore.*value.*3|inflightSemaphore.*3/\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[92.1, "o", "\r\nWith 3 frames in flight and only 2 luminance buffers: when encoding frame N, the GPU might still be executing frame N-1 (which writes to buffer `(N-1) % 2`). Frame N reads from buffer `(N-1) % 2`. If 3 frames can be in flight, then when encoding frame 3, frame 1 might still be executing. Frame 3 reads buffer `(3-1)%2 = 0`. Frame 2 writes buffer `(2-1)%2 = 0`... Wait, let me trace this more carefully.\r\n\r\nFrame 0: writes to buffer 0%2=0, reads nothing (first frame)\r\nFrame 1: writes to buffer 1%2=1, reads buffer 0%2=0 (from frame 0) -- needs frame 0 completed\r\nFrame 2: writes to buffer 2%2=0, reads buffer 1%2=1 (from frame 1) -- needs frame 1 completed\r\n\r\nWith 3 in flight: frames 0, 1, 2 can all be in flight simultaneously. When frame 2 encodes, it reads buffer 1, which is being written by frame 1's GPU work. Race condition. But wait, let me re-read the code: `frameCount` starts at 0. Let me trace frame count increments.\r\n\r\nActually, the `frameCount` is incremented *after* writing, so:\r\n- Frame call 0: hasGPUResult=false, writes to buffer 0, frameCount becomes 1\r\n- Frame call 1: reads buffer (1-1)%2=0, writes to buffer 1%2=1, frameCount becomes 2\r\n- Frame call 2: reads buffer (2-1)%2=1, writes to buffer 2%2=0, frameCount becomes 3\r\n\r\nWith 3 inflight frames, when frame call 2 is encoding, frame call 1's command buffer might still be executing on the GPU. Frame call 2 reads buffer index 1, which is the same buffer frame call 1 wrote to. If frame call 1's GPU work hasn't completed yet, the CPU read at frame call 2 is reading stale/incomplete data.\r\n\r\nHowever, in `makeFrameCallback`, the semaphore is waited on with `.now()` timeout (non-blocking). The semaphore is signaled in the completion handler. So actually the constraint is that at most 3 frames are in flight at a time. With double-buffering on the luminance buffers, there could be a read-before-write race when 3 frames are in flight.\r\n\r\nLet me also check if there's any notion of stopping the analysis drain.\r\n"]
[93.6, "o", "\u001b[2m  [81,808 ctx | 1 out]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[94.4, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u23bf Grep\u001b[0m \u001b[2m/analysisQueue.*cancel|analysisTimer|stopAnalysis|drainLoop/\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[94.55, "o", "\r\nThe analysis drain loop uses `[weak self]` and schedules via `self?.scheduleAnalysisDrain()`. If the orchestrator is deallocated, the weak reference becomes nil and the loop stops. But `stop()` doesn't stop the analysis loop explicitly -- it relies on deallocation. If `stop()` is called but the orchestrator isn't deallocated, the analysis loop continues running.\r\n\r\nNow I have enough information. Let me write the findings.\r\n"]
